    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Before we find H.C.F. and L.C.M., we need three simple ideas to become automatic:\n\nFactors.\nMultiples.\nPrime factorization.\n\nThink of a number as something that can be built in different ways.\n\nFor example, 12 can be made as:\n\n1 × 12\n2 × 6\n3 × 4\n\nThe numbers that divide 12 exactly are its factors.\n\nThese ideas are the building blocks we will use later for H.C.F. and L.C.M.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "factors_basics"
        }
      ]
    },
    "finish": {
      "id": "finish",
      "type": "COMPLETION",
      "content": {
        "text": "Great work. You now have the foundation for the next H.C.F. and L.C.M. ideas.\n\nFactor = a number that divides another number exactly.\n\nMultiple = a number obtained by multiplying another number by a whole number.\n\nPrime number = a number with exactly two factors: 1 and itself.\n\nPrime factorization = writing a number as a product of prime factors.\n\nPerfect number = the sum of its divisors, excluding itself, equals the number.\n\nOne mental picture to keep:\n\nFactors are the pieces that fit exactly into a number.\nMultiples are the numbers you reach by repeated multiplication.\nPrime factorization breaks a number down into its prime building blocks.\n\nThose prime building blocks are what we will use next to find H.C.F. and L.C.M.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": []
    },
