    "prime_remedy": {
      "id": "prime_remedy",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Use the definition, not a guess.\n\nA prime number has exactly two factors:\n1 and itself.\n\nFor example:\n\n4 has factors 1, 2 and 4 → not prime.\n9 has factors 1, 3 and 9 → not prime.\n15 has factors 1, 3, 5 and 15 → not prime.\n\n2 has only 1 and 2 → prime.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "prime_retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "factor_remedy": {
      "id": "factor_remedy",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's use one test only:\n\nA factor must divide the number with no remainder.\n\nFor 24:\n\n24 ÷ 4 = 6 ✓\n24 ÷ 5 = 4.8 ✗\n24 ÷ 7 leaves a remainder ✗\n24 ÷ 11 leaves a remainder ✗\n\nSo 4 is the correct factor.\n\nDon't ask whether a number simply looks related to 24. Ask: can 24 be divided by it exactly?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "factor_retry"
        }
      ]
    },
