    "prime_numbers": {
      "id": "prime_numbers",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Now we need the smallest building blocks: prime numbers.\n\nA prime number has exactly two factors: 1 and itself.\n\nExamples:\n\n2 → 1 and 2\n3 → 1 and 3\n5 → 1 and 5\n7 → 1 and 7\n\nThese numbers cannot be broken into smaller factors apart from 1 and themselves.\n\nA non-prime number can be broken further.\n\nFor example:\n\n12 = 2 × 6\n6 = 2 × 3\n\nSo:\n\n12 = 2 × 2 × 3\n\nNow every factor on the right is prime.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "prime_check"
        }
      ]
    },
    "factors_basics": {
      "id": "factors_basics",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's start with factors.\n\nIf a number divides another number exactly, without leaving a remainder, it is called a factor of that number.\n\nTake 12:\n\n12 ÷ 3 = 4\n\nNothing is left over, so 3 is a factor of 12.\n\nThe same is true for 1, 2, 4, 6 and 12.\n\nSo the factors of 12 are:\n\n1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12\n\nReal-world picture:\nImagine 12 chocolates. If they can be shared equally among 3 people with nothing left over, 3 is a factor of 12.\n\nThe key word is exactly.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "factor_check"
        }
      ]
    },
