    "perfect_number": {
      "id": "perfect_number",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "One final idea from this part of the chapter is the perfect number.\n\nA number is called perfect if the sum of all its divisors, excluding the number itself, is equal to the number.\n\nTake 6.\n\nThe divisors of 6 other than 6 are:\n\n1, 2, 3\n\nAdd them:\n\n1 + 2 + 3 = 6\n\nSo 6 is a perfect number.\n\nFor this idea, simply follow the definition:\n1. Find the divisors except the number itself.\n2. Add them.\n3. Check whether the sum equals the original number.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "perfect_check"
        }
      ]
    },
    "perfect_remedy": {
      "id": "perfect_remedy",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's apply the definition carefully.\n\nFor 6, leave 6 itself out.\n\nDivisors: 1, 2, 3\nSum: 1 + 2 + 3 = 6\n\nThe sum equals the original number, so 6 is perfect.\n\nThe common trap is including the number itself in the sum. The definition specifically excludes it.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "perfect_retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "multiple_basics": {
      "id": "multiple_basics",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Now turn the idea around.\n\nIf 3 divides 12 exactly, then 3 is a factor of 12 and 12 is a multiple of 3.\n\nMultiples are formed by multiplying a number by whole numbers:\n\n3 × 1 = 3\n3 × 2 = 6\n3 × 3 = 9\n3 × 4 = 12\n3 × 5 = 15\n\nSo 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, ... are multiples of 3.\n\nReal-world picture:\nThink of packs containing 3 items. One pack gives 3, two packs give 6, three packs give 9, and so on.\n\nSo:\nFactor → divides into the number exactly.\nMultiple → comes from multiplying the number.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "multiple_check"
        }
      ]
    },
    "multiple_remedy": {
      "id": "multiple_remedy",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Remember the direction of the relationship.\n\nIf 6 × 3 = 18, then:\n\n6 is a factor of 18.\n18 is a multiple of 6.\n\nA factor goes into a number exactly.\nA multiple is produced from the number by multiplication.\n\nFor 6, the first few multiples are 6, 12, 18, 24, ...",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "multiple_retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "factorization_330": {
      "id": "factorization_330",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's use a number from the chapter: 330.\n\nBreak it into smaller parts:\n\n330 = 33 × 10\n33 = 3 × 11\n10 = 2 × 5\n\nTherefore:\n\n330 = 2 × 3 × 5 × 11\n\nThis is why prime factorization is useful in H.C.F. and L.C.M. problems. It exposes the basic prime building blocks inside a number.\n\nThe order of the prime factors does not matter. What matters is that multiplying them gives the original number and every factor is prime.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "factorization_check"
        }
      ]
    },
