    "prime_factor_remedy": {
      "id": "prime_factor_remedy",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Prime factorization is not finished while a composite number is still present.\n\nFor 84:\n\n84 = 2 × 42\n= 2 × 2 × 21\n= 2 × 2 × 3 × 7\n\nSo 2 × 6 × 7 is not the final form because 6 is composite.\n\nThe final line must contain only prime numbers.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "prime_factor_retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "prime_factorization": {
      "id": "prime_factorization",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Now put the ideas together.\n\nPrime factorization means writing a number as a product of prime factors.\n\nTake 84:\n\n84 = 2 × 42\n42 = 2 × 21\n21 = 3 × 7\n\nSo:\n\n84 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 7\n\nThe final factors are all prime.\n\nReal-world picture:\nImagine a machine made from smaller parts. If one part is still made from smaller parts, we keep taking it apart until only the basic parts remain.\n\nPrime factorization does the same thing to a number.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "prime_factor_check"
        }
      ]
    },
    "factorization_remedy": {
      "id": "factorization_remedy",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Start from the number itself:\n\n330 = 33 × 10\n\nNow break both parts:\n\n33 = 3 × 11\n10 = 2 × 5\n\nSo:\n\n330 = 2 × 3 × 5 × 11\n\nCheck the final expression:\nEvery factor is prime, and the product is 330.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "factorization_retry"
        }
      ]
    },
