    "book_factorization_remedy": {
      "id": "book_factorization_remedy",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's break 1122 down carefully.\n\n1122 = 2 × 561\n561 = 3 × 187\n187 = 11 × 17\n\nTherefore:\n\n1122 = 2 × 3 × 11 × 17\n\nNotice that 9, 561 and 187 are not prime, so they cannot remain in the final prime factorization.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "book_factorization_retry"
        }
      ]
    },
    "book_factorization_examples": {
      "id": "book_factorization_examples",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The book uses this same prime-factor idea with several numbers.\n\nFor 1122:\n\n1122 = 2 × 561\n= 2 × 3 × 187\n= 2 × 3 × 11 × 17\n\nSo its prime factors are 2, 3, 11 and 17.\n\nAnother example is 252:\n\n252 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 3 × 7\n\nThese examples show the main habit: keep breaking the number until every factor left is prime.\n\nOnce the prime building blocks are visible, H.C.F. and L.C.M. questions become much easier to organize.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "book_factorization_check"
        }
      ]
    },
    "factor_multiple_relationship": {
      "id": "factor_multiple_relationship",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Here is the relationship in one line:\n\nIf a × b = c, then a and b are factors of c, and c is a multiple of both a and b.\n\nExample:\n\n4 × 5 = 20\n\nSo 4 and 5 are factors of 20.\n20 is a multiple of 4 and also a multiple of 5.\n\nThis simple relationship is the language used throughout H.C.F. and L.C.M.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "prime_numbers"
        }
      ]
    }
